Step 11 — Network Monitoring

Now we build the Network Monitoring Module.

The purpose is simple:

While Mininet traffic is running, measure what is happening in the network.

We need this data later for congestion detection, Braess detection, and route optimization.

Step 11 goal

We want to collect:

Latency
Throughput
Packet loss
Link utilization
Traffic/flow information

For Review 1, we don't need the final AI/Braess logic yet.

Step 11.1 — Create the monitoring file

Go to your project:

cd ~/medroute

Check:

pwd

Then create:

nano controller/network_monitor.py

Paste this:

import time
import subprocess

class NetworkMonitor:

    def ping_test(self, source, destination):
        command = ["ping", "-c", "4", destination]

        result = subprocess.run(
            command,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            text=True
        )

        output = result.stdout

        latency = None
        packet_loss = None

        for line in output.splitlines():
            if "packet loss" in line:
                packet_loss = line.split("%")[0].split()[-1]

            if "min/avg/max" in line:
                values = line.split("=")[1].split("/")
                latency = values[1]

        return latency, packet_loss

    def show_result(self, source, destination):
        latency, packet_loss = self.ping_test(source, destination)

        print("============================")
        print("MedRoute Network Monitor")
        print("============================")
        print("Source:", source)
        print("Destination:", destination)
        print("Average Latency:", latency, "ms")
        print("Packet Loss:", packet_loss, "%")
        print("============================")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    monitor = NetworkMonitor()
    monitor.show_result("h1", "10.0.0.2")

Save:

Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X

BUT don't run this yet.

There is one important issue.

This Python program runs in your WSL terminal, while h1 exists inside Mininet's network namespace.

So we should make the monitor work through Mininet, rather than trying to ping h1 directly from WSL.

For our project, the cleaner approach is to create a Mininet monitoring script that runs the measurements from the hosts.

Step 11.2 — Create the Mininet monitor

Run:

nano topology/network_monitor.py

Paste:

from mininet.net import Mininet
from mininet.node import OVSKernelSwitch, RemoteController
from mininet.link import TCLink
from mininet.log import setLogLevel

def run():

    net = Mininet(
        switch=OVSKernelSwitch,
        link=TCLink,
        controller=None
    )

    c0 = net.addController(
        "c0",
        controller=RemoteController,
        ip="127.0.0.1",
        port=6653
    )

    h1 = net.addHost("h1", ip="10.0.0.1")
    h2 = net.addHost("h2", ip="10.0.0.2")

    s1 = net.addSwitch("s1")
    s2 = net.addSwitch("s2")
    s3 = net.addSwitch("s3")
    s4 = net.addSwitch("s4")

    net.addLink(h1, s1)
    net.addLink(s1, s2)
    net.addLink(s2, s4)
    net.addLink(s1, s3)
    net.addLink(s3, s4)
    net.addLink(s4, h2)

    net.start()

    print("============================")
    print("MedRoute Network Monitoring")
    print("============================")

    print("\nPing Test:")
    print(h1.cmd("ping -c 4 10.0.0.2"))

    print("\nNetwork Interface Statistics:")
    print(h1.cmd("ifconfig"))
    print(h2.cmd("ifconfig"))

    net.stop()

if __name__ == "__main__":
    setLogLevel("info")
    run()

Save and exit.

